---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectPageHeader from '../../components/ProjectPageHeader.astro';
import ProjectSection from '../../components/ProjectSection.astro';
import ProjectList from '../../components/ProjectList.astro';
import ProjectProse from '../../components/ProjectProse.astro';
import TagList from '../../components/TagList.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Base title="Korpus / Kacper Noniewicz" description="Durable JSON store for my personal-data spine: JSON Schema validation, SQLite, and a Kor service with an HTTP+SSE gateway.">
  <div class="py-16 pb-20">
    <ProjectPageHeader label="Retired / Go / AGPL-3.0" title="Korpus" intro="The single writer for my personal-data spine. Korpus is a Kor service that validates every record against a JSON Schema, stores it in SQLite, and only acknowledges a write after it is committed." repo="https://github.com/knoniewicz/korpus" />

    <div class="max-w-180">
      <ProjectSection title="What it does">
        <ProjectList items={[
          "Validated upserts: korpus.write is acknowledged after the SQLite commit, so a caller can retry safely",
          "Services register the JSON Schema of their own types at startup; schemas are stored in the database",
          "One table per type, with indexed generated columns for the fields a schema lists in x-index",
          "Queries with where, order_by, limit and since, over Kor or HTTP",
          "Publishes <type>.written after each commit, also as Server-Sent Events for browsers",
          "Online backups with VACUUM INTO, safe while running",
        ]} />
      </ProjectSection>

      <ProjectSection title="Why I built it">
        <ProjectProse>
          <p>Every collector I ran was doing the same work: validating payloads, mapping them to tables, handling upserts and retries. Korpus puts that in one place, so the rest of the system only has to say what a record looks like and where it came from.</p>
          <p>The first version sat behind Redis and wrote to Postgres, with foreign-key-aware retries for records that arrived out of order. The current version dropped all of that: writes are acknowledged requests, ids are deterministic so a retry is an upsert, and relations are plain ids inside the JSON.</p>
        </ProjectProse>
      </ProjectSection>

      <ProjectSection title="How it works">
        <CodeBlock>Service → Kor (korpus.write) → JSON Schema → SQLite commit → ack + korpus.&lt;type&gt;.written</CodeBlock>
        <ProjectProse>
          <p>A write for a type nobody registered fails with <code class="font-mono text-sm">unknown_type</code>; an invalid payload fails with <code class="font-mono text-sm">invalid</code> before anything touches the database. Services that don't speak Kor use the same path over HTTP.</p>
        </ProjectProse>
      </ProjectSection>

      <ProjectSection title="Stack">
        <TagList tags={["Go", "SQLite", "JSON Schema", "Kor", "HTTP + SSE"]} />
      </ProjectSection>

      <ProjectSection title="Status">
        <ProjectProse>
          <p>Retired in November 2026. Korpus was the store behind my personal infrastructure until Bober replaced the whole spine with something smaller.</p>
        </ProjectProse>
      </ProjectSection>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
